==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Volta''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Gigavolta''' at level 30 using [[Catfruit]] and XP.

Evolves into '''Teravolta''' at level 60 using [[Behemoth Stone]]s, [[Catfruit]], and XP.

==Strategy/Usage==
A very solid anti-Alien crowd-controller, Voli can be stacked to easily perma[[Freeze|freeze]] Aliens which is super useful for both [[Into the Future]] and [[Cats of the Cosmos]]. Volta gains the extra damage but cannot easily permafreeze them as efficiently as the first form. Voli can be super useful in stages like [[The Wormhole Opens (Deadly)|The Wormhole Opens]], as permafreezing is easy with this cat. [[Black Hole Rising (Insane)|Black Hole Rising]] is another Cyclone Stage where Voli does extremely well. Its Evolved Form works excellently with [[Surfer Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Seafarer Cat]], and both can Freeze Alien enemies to a point where they are non-threats, however oftentimes Seafarer alone can be enough, so think carefully about whether spending the extra slot for Voli is worth it.

==='''True Form'''===
Gigavolta improves just enough to justify getting this True Form if you need it. The HP and Damage buffs are fairly inconsequential on a crowd-controller like it; whether Gigavolta deals 4.2k or 5.6K DPS, it hardly changes noteworthy interactions. However, its Freeze uptime is upgraded from ~50% to ~61% (~60 to ~74% with [[Fruit Treasures]]), which is the most noteworthy improvement, and is definitely noticeable in application. If you genuinely need it to be a better anti-Alien CC, Gigavolta could be a bang for your buck.

==='''Ultra Form'''===
Teravolta gains a two-part Long Distance [[Multi-Hit]] that extends its effective reach up to 520 range on second hit, at the cost of a small blind spot. This actually boosts its Freeze's uptime all the way up to ~85% for targets struck by both attacks, though they only overlap from 270-371 range. This means it can finally reach a 100% Freeze uptime on Aliens when aided by a few Freeze Up [[Cat Combo]]s. Teravolta also gains even more HP and Damage buffs, bringing it in line with late game Uber standards; while they're a welcome improvement, its stats are still not a very outstanding quality, especially given its mediocre standing range. While the unit appears "okay" in a vacuum, it's good to consider that most stages at this point in the game are littered with volatile enemies that have the potential to completely deny Teravolta's effective usage. It cannot withstand even a light tap from many non-targets in endgame, and it lacks any resistances to pierce whatsoever, rendering this poor pixie a sitting duck to any enemy looking to pick a fight from afar. A hyper-specialist crowd-controller with no other remarkable attributes is ill-suited to contend for your precious [[Catseye|Dark Catseyes]] and [[Catfruit|Behemoth Stones]] when many better Ultra Form options are usually readily available. Only truly invest if you have spare [[Catseye|Dark Catseyes]] leftover and unlocked the Ultra Forms and Ultra Talents of units with higher priority. 

[[Immune to Drain|Drain Immunity]] is also a wash since Teravolta lacks Sage Slayer, leaving the unit fairly vulnerable against [[Sage of Selection Darvin]] in [[Endless Forms Most Beautiful]]. It can help during the [[Commander Ostromeda]] phases of the fight, since Teravolta will be off cooldown every cycle thanks to the immunity, but that's really all it does.

==='''Talents'''===
Gigavolta gets various immunities for his Talents. As a result, they only improve very specific interactions and tend to do absolutely nothing for most others. They are generally not recommended as a result, but unlocking all three of them notably counters [[Haniwanwan]] and [[Golem Sunfish]] for whatever that's worth.

'''[[Immune to Curse]]''' is intended to help in mixed Relic stages, however, the best solution to avoid getting Cursed is to avoid taking damage entirely. In Gigavolta's case, the Relics that already outrange him are butchering him too quickly for the immunity to realistically matter, which makes this Talent only really good as safety net if it's struck by a hurdling [[Sir Rel]]. For 100 NP, a situational safety net like this is not worth it. The most notable use case for this Talent outside of Relics is in Endless Forms Most Beautiful, where Teravolta retains its anti-Alien abilities against Sage of Selection Darvin.

'''[[Immune to Slow]]''' makes it so you don't need to time it against [[Queen B]], [[Croakley]], and [[Crustaceous Scissorex]]. Truthfully, he has enough speed for this investment to simply be unnecessary in most situations. There are a few niche enemies that it could help against, namely in [[Great Angel Chibinel]] and [[Super Hyppoh]], but these aren't relevant enough to reasonably consider.

'''[[Immune to Freeze]]''' is a much more oppressive ability than Slow, and actually has a good set of matchup improvements to make it potentially worth unlocking in dire straits. It completely counters [[I.M. Phace]], [[Maawth]], and [[Henry]] even when tanking direct hits. In spite of it being Gigavolta's best immunity, it does not justify its NP cost when planning around said problematic enemies usually suffices well enough.

'''Attack Buff''' and '''Defense Buff''' are not very worthwhile if you're not planning on unlocking Gigavolta's Ultra Form, since its stats still leave plenty to be desired after the hefty NP sink. Even with the Ultra Form where its stats improve considerably, you're probably better off spending the NP somewhere else.

